{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"856731614c4faa6a","name":"card_event_happy_time","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_happy_time","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"HAPPY TIME","text":"Choose 1 of 3 cards in the enemy hand. Copy it, then Convert both into PLAN.","flavorText":"The Happy Time (German: Die Glückliche Zeit) refers to a period from July 1940 to early 1941 when German U-boats inflicted severe losses on Allied shipping in the Atlantic. After France fell in June 1940, Germany gained access to Atlantic ports like Lorient and Brest, allowing U-boats to operate farther west and remain at sea longer. Operating primarily against poorly defended merchant convoys, U-boats sank ships at an alarming rate. At the peak of this campaign, Allied shipping losses reached over 300,000 tons per month. German commanders like Günther Prien, Otto Kretschmer, and Joachim Schepke became national heroes. Kretschmer alone sank over 200,000 tons during this phase, often by surfacing at night and attacking with deck guns to conserve torpedoes. The British were hampered by a lack of escort vessels and effective convoy tactics. Radar and sonar were limited, and air cover did not extend far enough into the Atlantic to protect convoys. The tide began to turn by early 1941 as the Royal Navy improved its escort systems and tactics. The entry into service of more escort ships and the breaking of German naval codes helped reduce shipping losses, bringing an end to what German U-boat crews referred to as \"the Happy Time\".","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_happy_time","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_happy_time_title","SourceString":"HAPPY TIME","LocalizedString":"HAPPY TIME"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_happy_time_text","SourceString":"Choose 1 of 3 cards in the enemy hand. Copy it, then Convert both into PLAN.","LocalizedString":"Choose 1 of 3 cards in the enemy hand. Copy it, then Convert both into PLAN."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_happy_time_history","SourceString":"The Happy Time (German: Die Glückliche Zeit) refers to a period from July 1940 to early 1941 when German U-boats inflicted severe losses on Allied shipping in the Atlantic. After France fell in June 1940, Germany gained access to Atlantic ports like Lorient and Brest, allowing U-boats to operate farther west and remain at sea longer. Operating primarily against poorly defended merchant convoys, U-boats sank ships at an alarming rate. At the peak of this campaign, Allied shipping losses reached over 300,000 tons per month. German commanders like Günther Prien, Otto Kretschmer, and Joachim Schepke became national heroes. Kretschmer alone sank over 200,000 tons during this phase, often by surfacing at night and attacking with deck guns to conserve torpedoes. The British were hampered by a lack of escort vessels and effective convoy tactics. Radar and sonar were limited, and air cover did not extend far enough into the Atlantic to protect convoys. The tide began to turn by early 1941 as the Royal Navy improved its escort systems and tactics. The entry into service of more escort ships and the breaking of German naval codes helped reduce shipping losses, bringing an end to what German U-boat crews referred to as \"the Happy Time\".","LocalizedString":"The Happy Time (German: Die Glückliche Zeit) refers to a period from July 1940 to early 1941 when German U-boats inflicted severe losses on Allied shipping in the Atlantic. After France fell in June 1940, Germany gained access to Atlantic ports like Lorient and Brest, allowing U-boats to operate farther west and remain at sea longer. Operating primarily against poorly defended merchant convoys, U-boats sank ships at an alarming rate. At the peak of this campaign, Allied shipping losses reached over 300,000 tons per month. German commanders like Günther Prien, Otto Kretschmer, and Joachim Schepke became national heroes. Kretschmer alone sank over 200,000 tons during this phase, often by surfacing at night and attacking with deck guns to conserve torpedoes. The British were hampered by a lack of escort vessels and effective convoy tactics. Radar and sonar were limited, and air cover did not extend far enough into the Atlantic to protect convoys. The tide began to turn by early 1941 as the Royal Navy improved its escort systems and tactics. The entry into service of more escort ships and the breaking of German naval codes helped reduce shipping losses, bringing an end to what German U-boat crews referred to as \"the Happy Time\"."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Convert"],"draftBaseWeight":0.9,"spawnCardName":"card_event_plan","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'WolfPack_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/WolfPack_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/NavalWarfare/t_happy_time.t_happy_time","SubPathString":""},"artist":"German War Poster","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/HAPPY_TIME.HAPPY_TIME","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":327.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_happy_time_photo","SourceString":"German U-boats U-123 and U-201 departing Lorient, France, June 1941.","LocalizedString":"German U-boats U-123 and U-201 departing Lorient, France, June 1941."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"PossibleCards","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardsToConvert","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"